  "member_id": "linnarsson-sten",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/medicine_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B005",
        "laureates": [
          "Kazutoshi Mori",
          "Peter Walter"
        ],
        "citation": "for discoveries of the unfolded protein response and signaling from the endoplasmic reticulum"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B009",
    "B022"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B005 remains my preferred configuration because it identifies a fundamental, conserved cellular signaling system linking endoplasmic-reticulum protein-folding stress to adaptive responses. The achievement is mechanistically mature, broadly consequential for physiology and disease, and can be represented by a compact pair whose complementary contributions to central UPR signaling are coherent. Unlike several highly important translational candidates, the scientific discovery boundary is principally a biological mechanism rather than a long, distributed development pathway.",
    "case_against": "The strongest reservation is that UPR research spans numerous influential discoveries and investigators, so a two-person award requires careful historical delimitation of the foundational signaling advances rather than recognition of the whole proteostasis field.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "perlmann-thomas",
        "point": "Perlmann ranks leptin first, emphasizing its decisive endocrine framework for appetite and body-weight regulation, while also placing B005 second.",
        "response": "I agree that leptin is a foundational and clearly attributable physiological discovery. Its more limited therapeutic reach in common obesity, however, makes its overall medical consequence less decisive to me than the deeply established and broadly explanatory UPR mechanism."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "svenningsson-per",
        "point": "Svenningsson ranks GLP-1 physiology first because of its exceptional therapeutic benefit in diabetes and obesity, while noting difficult boundaries between foundational physiology and later drug development.",
        "response": "The clinical impact of GLP-1 therapies is extraordinary, but that very success also exposes a diffuse attribution chain spanning peptide biology, physiology, pharmacology, and development. B005 currently offers the more sharply delimited, mature mechanistic achievement and a more defensible compact attribution."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "wahren-herlenius-marie",
        "point": "Wahren-Herlenius gives priority to TNF blockade for its transformative clinical effect in inflammatory disease and notes the wider contribution network behind therapy development.",
        "response": "TNF blockade is a compelling alternative with durable patient benefit. I give B005 the edge because the proposed laureates map more directly onto an integrated foundational discovery, whereas the TNF discovery-to-therapy pathway appears harder to delimit among the broader contributing network."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether further historical review would confirm that Mori and Walter alone best delimit the foundational UPR signaling discovery.",
      "Whether the committee should place greater weight on the immediate clinical transformation represented by GLP-1 physiology, TNF blockade, or molecular diagnostics."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
